    \u0430 \u041b\u0435\u0432 \u0422\u043e\u043b\u0441\u0442\u043e\u0439 \u043d\u0430\u0437\u044b\u0432\u0430\u043b
    \u0435\u0433\u043e \u00ab\u0443\u0447\u0438\u0442\u0435\u043b\u0435\u043c \u0447\u0435\u043b\u043e\u0432\u0435\u0447\u0435\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0430\u00bb."
  shortBioEn: "He is a man of the era whose short but incredibly rich life has brought both genius scientific insight and deep spiritual crisis and the creation of immortal literary and philosophical works, and he was not just a scientist, but, according to the modern-day definition, \"thinking cane\", whose creativity has become one of the most vivid evidences of the 17th century's rationalist crisis.\n\nThe family was born on June 19, 1623 in Claremont-Ferran, in the family of Etienne Pascal, the royal adviser and president of the tax administration, and Antoinette Begon.In 1631, the family moved to Paris, and the father, Etienne, the most respected mathematician, decided to take full responsibility for the education of the children without trusting the school; he developed a strict programme for the son that gave priority to ancient languages (Latin and Greek), and mathematics, which the father considered to be too exciting, was banned until the age of 15; however, as is often the case with genius, the ban only gave rise to interest.According to the sister of Pascal, Gilberta, 12-year-old Bles, without knowing any axiom, alone, with coal on the floor, \"opened\" the 32nd Euclid theorem of the size of the triangle.At the age of 16, inspired by Desargue's work, Pascal wrote \"Exai pour les coniques\" (Essai pour les coniques), where he formulated his famous theorem of mystical six-pervertiser, one of the pillars of design geometry. The achievement was so remarkable that, when he saw the manuscript, Descartes refused to believe that its author could be a teenager and assumed that it was the work of the father.\n\nIn 1639, Etienne Pascal was appointed an intendan (tax collector) to Rouan, and the family moved there.Pascal built about 50 prototypes before he drove the car to his head. Although the commercial success of the \"pascalina\" was not because of the high cost, the principle of the wheel-bound wheels was the basis of three centuries in advance. In Rouan, Pascal's first conversion to religion took place in 1646. After his father broke his hip, he was treated by two doctors who were convinced of the Yansenists.Jansenism is a strict flow in Catholicism, close to Augustine's ideas of pre-determination and saving grace, and under their influence Pascal, and then his entire family, it's coming across this harsh doctrine, and at the same time it's being captured by physical experiments. It repeats Torricelli's experiments with a mercury barometer, proving the existence of atmospheric pressure and the possibility of voids, a concept that the Aristothelians have rejected.In a famous experience on Mount Pui de Dom (at Pascal's request, by his son-in-law, Floran Perieu), he showed that the pressure of the air was decreasing with altitude, which finally confirmed Torricelli's theory. On the basis of these experiments, he formulated a basic hydrostatic law (Pascal Act) on the transmission of pressure in fluids and invented a syringe and a hydraulic press.\n\nIn 1647, Pascal moved to Paris, where he met Descartes, and their communication was brief but tense: they did not agree on the void and the methodology of science. Pascal, unlike Decart, considered the experiment to be a priority. After his father's death in 1651, Pascal was in crisis.He is close to the aristocratic secular environment, particularly the Duke de Ruanne, and regular salons such as Chevalier de Mere and Damien Mitton, from which he heard the famous \"striking of the stake-sharing challenge\": How is it fair to separate the bank if the game is interrupted early? — Pascal, in correspondence with Pierre Ferma in 1654, finds a solution, thus laying the foundation for mathematical probability theory.During the same period, he wrote \"Tactat on the arithmetic triangle\", which describes a famous table of binominal coefficients, now known as the Pascal triangle.\n\n♪ The night of fire and the journey to Por Royal despite the glory and secular advances, internal anxiety and illness (from the age of 18 he was in severe headaches) did not leave Pascal. His spiritual quest culminated in the night of 23 to 24 November 1654, and in a state of mystical insight, he experienced \"second treatment\" — a total, final appeal to God. The shock was so strong that he recorded a parchment that he had been tested on a piece of parchment.This document, called \"Memorial\", began with the word \"Feu\" and contained lines of personal revelation; until the death of Pascal, he carried this note, which was sewn into the camsol lining; in January 1655, he was removed to the Poor Royal monastery, the center of the Jansenism; he did not accept the nun's haircut, but he became one of the leading intellectuals and polemists of the movement.\n\nThe main work: \"Pyjima\" and \"Poals\" in Por Royala is the literary and polythetic talent of Pascal. 1. \"Write to the province\" (1656 - 1657) is a series of 18 satirical letters written under the pseudonym Louis de Montalt, in which Pascal smugly, sarcastic witty and logical accuracy destroyed the moral jesuitistics who, according to the Yansenists, were far too easy to release the sins of the rich sinners.This pamphlet was not only a religious document, but also a masterpiece of French prose, a model of satirical style that influenced the development of all national literature. 2. \"Thinks\" is Pascal's main philosophy work, which he worked on until his death.After his death, he had no time to put his records into order. After his death, the scattered notes and aphorisms were collected and published under the name \"Thinks.\" It was in \"Thinks\" that the tragic and magnificent image of the man, lost between the two abysses — the infinity of the universe and his own nothingness.Pascal has first raised the question of the limits of the mind with such a sharpness, and he has opposed it to the logic of the heart, which is the intuitive realization of the first principles of existence.In fact, it is a pragmatic argument for faith based on the theory of probability, to which he himself has put his hand.\n\nIn recent years Pascal has suffered from a severe, painful illness, and in recent years he has been so weak that he has been unable to move, but he has continued to do so: he has designed a new form of public transport (omnibus) for the poor, has participated in scientific discussions, and he died in Paris on 19 August 1662, at the age of 39, in pain of an incurable disease.His departure was filled with Christian humility and dignity, and centuries later his ideas about the paradox of human existence, about \"the logic of the heart\" were accepted by existential philosophers, and Lev Tolstoy called him \"the teacher of mankind\"."
  interestingFactsRu: "\u0417\u0430 \u0441\u0432\u043e\u044e \u043d\u0435\u0434\u043e\u043b\u0433\u0443\u044e,
